From: Leszek Swirski <leszeks@chromium.org>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 15:52:10 +0200
Subject: [ast] Normalize one-byte content in AstValueFactory and StringTable

When internalizing a two-byte string that is a valid array index in
StringTable::LookupString, StringHasher::HashSequentialString did not
set *out_one_byte_content = true in the TryParseArrayIndex fast path.
Additionally, if the string already had a computed hash field,
LookupString skipped EnsureRawHash and left one_byte_content as false.
Consequently, two-byte array-index strings were internalized as
TwoByteInternalizedString instead of being canonicalized to one-byte.

When AstValueFactory::GetString imported such a string into an
AstRawString during lazy compilation, it created a two-byte
AstRawString. AstRawString::AsArrayIndex then attempted to parse the
uncached array index (> 7 digits) using OneByteStringStream on raw
uint16_t storage, failing due to interleaved null bytes and causing a
property count mismatch between BuildObjectLiteral and the boilerplate
descriptor.

Fix this by:
1. Setting *out_one_byte_content = true for array index hashes in
   StringHasher::HashSequentialString, and setting one_byte_content =
   true for pre-hashed integer-index strings in
   StringTable::LookupString.
2. Normalizing two-byte strings with one-byte content to one-byte in
   AstValueFactory::GetString, and asserting DCHECK(is_one_byte()) in
   AstRawString::AsArrayIndex.

